  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 305 AM CDT Sat Aug 29 2026\n\nConfidence remains high that upper-level ridging will drive our \nlocal weather through the upcoming work week. Guidance consensus \nremains steady in that the axis of this ridge will be roughly \ncentered along the Mississippi Valley, keeping an area of high \npressure at the surface centered over the Southeast. The positioning \nof both of these features will keep deep south-southwesterly flow \nover the Middle Mississippi Valley from Monday through at least \nFriday. During that time, 850mb temperatures will hover in the low \nto mid 20s degrees C, climatologically favoring surface temperatures \nin the mid to upper 90s degrees F. Confidence in this heat is high \ngiven the aforementioned continued consensus among guidance and that \nsurface flow will at times be southwesterly. This flow will allow \nfor adiabatic warming off of the Ozark Plateau, providing a boost to \ntemperatures and a drop in dew points particularly for locations \nacross central and southeastern Missouri. Highs reaching 100 degrees \ncan't be ruled out, with the NBM continuing to have roughly a 30% \nchance of this happening - particularly within the St. Louis metro \narea. Highs of this magnitude will come close to records, with \novernight lows having even a greater potential for breaking max low \nrecords. Please see the Climate section for more details. \n\nEven with this degree of heat, confidence remains low in the need \nfor heat headlines. The most likely scenario is that at least \nportions of the area see a Heat Advisory thanks to 4 or more days of \n100+ degree heat index values. While there are many locations that \ncurrently reach those criteria in our current forecast, this hinges \non dew points not trending any lower than they currently are. The \nNBM and many guidance sources typically do not handle local \ntemperatures and dew points well on days with southwesterly surface \nflow in place. Given this and that bias-corrected guidance is being \ninfluenced by the higher humidity we've experienced the last couple \nof months, it may not be until we experience a day or two of this \npattern before we are able to confidently say how humid the air\nmass will be the rest of the week.",
